  "dom_id": "step-q319377",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "The quartic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=-1,$ and ${x}_{4}=3$ and the coefficient of the quartic term is $2.$ Determine the **expanded form** of $P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,6$",
  "worked_solution": "Since our quartic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=-1,$ and ${x}_{4}=3,$ and the coefficient of the quartic term is $a=2,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) & =2(x-(-1))(x-(-1))(x-(-1))(x-3) \\\\  & =2(x+1)(x+1)(x+1)(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+1)(x+1)(x+1)(x-3) & =2({x}^{2}+2x+1)({x}^{2}-2x-3) \\\\  & =2({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-6x+{x}^{2}-2x-3) \\\\  & =2({x}^{4}-0{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-8x-3) \\\\  & =2{x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}-16x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=2{x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}-16x-6.$",
